=E2=86=92 uptime by operator<br>
=E2=86=92 outage survival<br>
=E2=86=92 charger-quality ranking<br>
=E2=86=92 site saturation<br>
=E2=86=92 location scarcity<br>
=E2=86=92 site-selection / routing decisions</p>
<p>If a feed has no useful transformation or action route, do not collect i=
t simply because it changes.</p>



3. Do not over-normalize too early
<p>Trying to force every source into one giant ontology can slow developmen=
t and destroy source-specific information.</p>
<p>Keep three layers:</p>

RAW
<p>Exact source data, immutable.</p>

UNIVERSAL CORE
<p>Only genuinely shared fields:</p>
<ul>
<li>entity_id</li>
<li>entity_type</li>
<li>source</li>
<li>observed_at</li>
<li>effective_at</li>
<li>place</li>
<li>event_type</li>
<li>provenance</li>
<li>confidence</li>
</ul>

DOMAIN TABLES
<p>Keep domain-specific structure intact.</p>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
<li>Qubic epoch fields stay compute-specific</li>
<li>EVSE status fields stay EV-specific</li>
<li>apprenticeship fields stay skills-specific</li>
</ul>
<p>Only unify what is genuinely common.</p>



4. Identity resolution is likely harder than collection
<p>POW will eventually need to resolve:</p>
<ul>
<li>person</li>
<li>installer business</li>
<li>Companies House entity</li>
<li>certification record</li>
<li>UPRN</li>
<li>grid node</li>
<li>vehicle model</li>
<li>exact device model</li>
<li>manufacturer part number</li>
<li>marketplace listing</li>
</ul>
<p>A wrong join is worse than no join because it produces confident-looking=
 bad advice.</p>
<p>Every identity edge must carry:</p>
<p>CONFIRMED<br>
INFERRED<br>
UNKNOWN</p>
<p>plus:</p>
<ul>
<li>match method</li>
<li>evidence</li>
<li>confidence</li>
<li>valid_from</li>
<li>valid_to</li>
<li>source</li>
</ul>
<p>Never silently merge entities.</p>



5. Licensing can poison the lake
<p>Publicly viewable does not automatically mean:</p>
<ul>
<li>retain forever</li>
<li>transform commercially</li>
<li>redistribute</li>
<li>expose via MCP/API</li>
</ul>
<p>This matters especially for:</p>
<ul>
<li>marketplaces</li>
<li>repair content</li>
<li>distributor APIs</li>
<li>company integrations</li>
</ul>
<p>Every source manifest should track:</p>
<ul>
<li>raw retention allowed?</li>
<li>retention period?</li>
<li>commercial use?</li>
<li>redistribution?</li>
<li>derived output allowed?</li>
<li>attribution?</li>
<li>API caching limits?</li>
</ul>
<p>Partition the raw lake according to rights policy.</p>
<p>Do not let one badly licensed source contaminate a derived product.</p>



6. R2 can become a dumping ground
<p>Raw-first is correct only if the storage system remains disciplined.</p>
<p>Required:</p>
<ul>
<li>source manifests</li>
<li>hashes</li>
<li>schema version</li>
<li>collector version</li>
<li>collection-run ledger</li>
<li>health checks</li>
<li>gap detection</li>
<li>compaction</li>
<li>Parquet tables</li>
<li>periodic restoration tests</li>
</ul>
<p>Bad outcome to avoid:</p>
<p>In 2027 we discover six months of data were silently malformed after an =
upstream schema change.</p>
<p>Collector reliability is part of the moat.</p>



7. Outcome data must arrive earlier
<p>Almost everything POW starts with is observational.</p>
<p>That is useful, but the deepest moat is:</p>
<p>POW prediction<br>
=E2=86=92 action<br>
=E2=86=92 intervention<br>
=E2=86=92 actual result</p>
<p>Ten genuine repairs with:</p>
<ul>
<li>predicted fault</li>
<li>predicted part</li>
<li>predicted time</li>
<li>predicted cost</li>
<li>actual part</li>
<li>actual time</li>
<li>actual cost</li>
<li>realised resale</li>
</ul>
<p>may be more strategically informative than another ten static datasets.<=
/p>
<p>Do not leave OutcomeGraph until the distant future.</p>
<p>Get to first receipts early.</p>



8. Muse can eventually call many live APIs itself
<p>Therefore this is NOT the durable moat:</p>
<p>=E2=80=9CPOW has an MCP for government data.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>The durable moat is:</p>
<p>historical state</p>
<ul>
<li>identity continuity</li>
<li>proprietary transformations</li>
<li>verified outcomes</li>
<li>accumulated error/correction history</li>
</ul>
<p>If a feature can be reproduced by Muse making three fresh API calls, tre=
at it as distribution/interface, not moat.</p>



9. Avoid premature sub-brand proliferation
<p>These names make sense:</p>
<ul>
<li>mining.pow.systems</li>
<li>repair.pow.systems</li>
<li>grid.pow.systems</li>
<li>ev.pow.systems</li>
<li>parts.pow.systems</li>
<li>skills.pow.systems</li>
<li>home.pow.systems</li>
</ul>
<p>But do not create:</p>
<ul>
<li>seven separate repos</li>
<li>seven MCP servers</li>
<li>seven social accounts</li>
<li>seven design systems</li>
<li>seven independent infrastructures</li>
</ul>
<p>For now they should mostly be namespaces/views over one POW platform.</p=
>
<p>Split operationally only when independent users, workflows or revenue ju=
stify it.</p>
<p><strong>Split products, not data.</strong></p>



10. Content must stay downstream of POW
<p>The content plan works only if content remains a projection of the garde=
n.</p>
<p>Rule:</p>
<p>No POW video unless it originates from:</p>
<ul>
<li>a stored transformation/evidence pack</li>
<li>a meaningful state change</li>
<li>a foundational explainer tied to POW</li>
</ul>
<p>Otherwise content research starts consuming attention again.</p>
<p>Canonical flow:</p>
<p>collector<br>
=E2=86=92 transformation<br>
=E2=86=92 evidence pack<br>
=E2=86=92 content candidate<br>
=E2=86=92 existing /content system<br>
=E2=86=92 publish<br>
=E2=86=92 audience response<br>
=E2=86=92 product/topic prioritisation</p>
<p>Content is a distribution and feedback layer, not another research organ=
isation.</p>



11. Avoid UK overfitting in the kernel
<p>UK is an excellent starting jurisdiction.</p>
<p>But the core POW schema should remain jurisdiction-neutral.</p>
<p>Prefer:</p>
<ul>
<li>jurisdiction_id</li>
<li>regulator_id</li>
<li>property_id</li>
<li>grid_node_id</li>
<li>certification_scheme_id</li>
</ul>
<p>rather than hard-coding assumptions such as UPRN into universal core obj=
ects.</p>
<p>UKGraph should implement the UK adapters.</p>
<p>POW should still work later in:</p>
<ul>
<li>India</li>
<li>EU</li>
<li>US</li>
<li>other jurisdictions</li>
</ul>



12. The five probes should be measured by plants, not reports
<p>Hourly probes can generate endless clever research without improving the=
 garden.</p>
<p>Their performance metrics should be:</p>
<ul>
<li>new source verified</li>
<li>collector running</li>
<li>first raw object stored</li>
<li>24h uninterrupted</li>
<li>first event emitted</li>
<li>first transformation computed</li>
<li>first join validated</li>
<li>first decision improved</li>
<li>first outcome received</li>
</ul>
<p>A week with three production-quality collectors is better than 500 pages=
 of reconnaissance.</p>



13. Recommended development posture

BROAD COLLECTION
<p>Only where:</p>
<ul>
<li>cheap</li>
<li>legal/permissioned</li>
<li>irreversible</li>
<li>likely useful later</li>
</ul>

NARROW TRANSFORMATION
<p>Only where:</p>
<ul>
<li>decision value is clear</li>
<li>evidence is measurable</li>
<li>the transformation is actually proprietary/useful</li>
</ul>

VERY NARROW EXECUTION
<p>One closed loop at a time.</p>

OUTCOMES
<p>As early as possible.</p>



14. First three domain clusters
<p>If sequencing now:</p>

1. Repair / Parts
<p>Why:</p>
<ul>
<li>direct =C2=A3 outcomes</li>
<li>clear transaction loop</li>
<li>useful to ordinary people</li>
<li>strong future robotics path</li>
<li>immediate first-party outcome collection</li>
</ul>
<p>Core loop:</p>
<p>asset<br>
=E2=86=92 fault<br>
=E2=86=92 part<br>
=E2=86=92 compatibility<br>
=E2=86=92 stock<br>
=E2=86=92 procedure<br>
=E2=86=92 user skill<br>
=E2=86=92 repair economics<br>
=E2=86=92 action<br>
=E2=86=92 outcome</p>

2. Compute / Mining
<p>Why:</p>
<ul>
<li>PowPowPow already exists</li>
<li>historical state collection is already underway</li>
<li>clean opportunity-cost modelling</li>
<li>easy to compare mine / rent / inference / sell / idle</li>
</ul>

3. Grid / EV
<p>Why:</p>
<ul>
<li>unusually strong temporal data</li>
<li>geographic physical constraints</li>
<li>EV live status is genuinely ephemeral</li>
<li>joins naturally into houses, solar, batteries, installers and energy</l=
i>
</ul>
<p>Skills, property, legislation and apprenticeships should initially enric=
h these clusters rather than becoming four more products.</p>



15. Repo-level priority test
<p>Put this at the top of the POW repo:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Does this work help POW remember something unrecoverable, comput=
e something meaningfully better, or cause a real-world action whose outcome=
 we can learn from?</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the answer is no to all three, it is probably not priority work.</p>
<p>Use three labels on every issue/PR:</p>

MEMORY
<p>Preserves state that will otherwise disappear.</p>

INTELLIGENCE
<p>Creates a transformation/join that improves a real decision.</p>

ACTION
<p>Causes an intervention and lets us observe the outcome.</p>
<p>A task should ideally satisfy at least one, and the best tasks satisfy t=
wo or three.</p>



16. Working doctrine
<p><strong>Capture first. Normalize second. Productize last.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Collect broadly where history is irreversible. Productize narrow=
ly.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Split products, not data.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Do not confuse public data access with moat.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Do not confuse historical data with valuable history.</strong></=
p>
<p><strong>Do not silently resolve uncertain identity.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Do not build sub-brands before their workflows earn independence=
.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Do not let content become a separate research project.</strong><=
/p>
<p><strong>Do not let probes optimize for report volume.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Get to outcomes early.</strong></p>
<p>The strategic goal is not merely to build a map of the physical economy.=
</p>
<p>It is to build a system that:</p>
<p><strong>remembers what reality looked like, understands what could be do=
ne, acts when useful, and learns from what happened next.</strong></p></div=
